<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Iqbal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Allamah, Hakim ul-Ummat, Mufakkir-e-Pakistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Muhammad Iqbal was an Islamic philosopher and poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, among the most influential Muslim thinkers of the 20th century. Born in Sialkot, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, he studied at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, qualified as a barrister at Lincoln's Inn and earned a PhD in philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1908, and he wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Persian such works as Asrar-e-Khudi and Bang-e-Dara. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> Address of 1930 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> he set out a political framework for the Muslim-majority regions of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, established in 1947, nine years after his death, honours him as its national poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal.jpg" alt="Muhammad Iqbal" width="330" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iqbal Academy Pakistan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allama_Iqbal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Iqbal came from a Punjabi family of Kashmiri ancestry and studied at Government College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, where he took his Master of Arts in 1899. He taught Arabic at the Oriental College, Lahore, and then philosophy at Government College. Encouraged by his teacher Sir Thomas Arnold, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1905, took a second Bachelor of Arts at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and was called to the bar at Lincoln's Inn. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he studied under Friedrich Hommel, and his thesis, The Development of Metaphysics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1908.</p><p>On his return he practised before the Chief Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, where more than 100 reported judgements bear his name, but he gave most of his time to writing. He joined the Punjab Muslim League as one of its first joint secretaries, was elected to the Punjab Legislative Council in 1927 and criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> as dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. He became convinced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> alone could keep the League united, and corresponded with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Of his roughly 12,000 verses, about 7,000 are in Persian. Asrar-i-Khudi (Secrets of the Self, 1915) sets out his idea of khudi, the self, whose aim is self-realisation and which passes through stages toward becoming a vice-regent of God. Reynold Alleyne Nicholson's translation introduced his writing to Western readers. His first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> collection, Bang-e-Dara (1924), has three phases: patriotic and nature poems to 1905, poems on European society written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and poems on the heritage of the Muslim community, addressed to the Ummah.</p><p>His six English lectures, delivered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, Hyderabad and Aligarh, appeared in 1930 as The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. In the same year, in the Allahabad Address to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>, he outlined a political framework for the Muslim-majority regions of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the late 1930s he debated nationalism with Hussain Ahmad Madani, who defended a culturally plural India while Iqbal looked first to a Muslim community in Muslim-majority lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a throat illness that began in 1933, Iqbal stopped practising law in 1934 and received a pension from the Nawab of Bhopal. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 21 April 1938 and is buried in Hazuri Bagh, between the Badshahi Mosque and the Lahore Fort.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> he is honoured as national poet, and 9 November, his birthday, is a public holiday. Many institutions bear his name, among them Allama Iqbal Open University and Allama Iqbal International Airport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> he is known as Iqbal of Lahore, and the Indonesian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> was among his admirers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Iqbal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture the national poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> shaped Urdu letters, Abdus Salam won the Nobel Prize in Physics and Malala Yousafzai became the youngest Nobel laureate; Pakistan launched the first satellite of any Muslim nation in 1990 and has won every major ICC cricket tournament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 the poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> proposed a consolidated Muslim state in north-western India, and in the Lahore Resolution of March 1940 the League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah demanded “independent states” for the Muslim-majority areas, holding that Hindus and Muslims were two nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Supporters include <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b>, who called it the first throb of life in modern Islam while noting that its vision of the past was uncritical, and Taha Hussein, who credited it with awakening the Arab soul.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In British India the Khilafat movement of 1919–24 expressed Muslims’ hopes for the caliphate, and <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b>, who in 1930 outlined a Muslim state in north-western India, influenced Maududi.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> visited in 1931–32 and wrote a poem, &quot;The Mosque of Cordoba&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Sare Jahan se Accha&quot;, formally &quot;Tarānah-e-Hindi&quot; (&quot;Anthem of the People of Hindustan&quot;), is an Urdu patriotic song for children written by the poet <b>Muhammad Iqbal</b> in the ghazal style of Urdu poetry.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sialkot, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.5,74.53&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Sialkot on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6464 982 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad Iqbal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6914 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6914 1252h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.5,74.53&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Personality is a state of tension and can continue only if that state is maintained. If the state of tension is not maintained, relaxation will ensue.&#8221;</p><footer>Statement of his philosophy, in R. A. Nicholson's introduction to The Secrets of the Self (1920) &#183; original &#183; written for his translator, who gave it &quot;in his own words as nearly as possible&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57317/pg57317-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That which fortifies personality is good, that which weakens it is bad.&#8221;</p><footer>Statement of his philosophy, in R. A. Nicholson's introduction to The Secrets of the Self (1920) &#183; original &#183; written for his translator, who gave it &quot;in his own words as nearly as possible&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57317/pg57317-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Physically as well as spiritually man is a self-contained centre, but he is not yet a complete individual. The greater his distance from God, the less his individuality. He who comes nearest to God is the completest person.&#8221;</p><footer>Statement of his philosophy, in R. A. Nicholson's introduction to The Secrets of the Self (1920) &#183; original &#183; written for his translator, who gave it &quot;in his own words as nearly as possible&quot; &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57317/pg57317-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The moral and religious ideal of man is not self-negation but self-affirmation, and he attains to this ideal by becoming more and more individual, more and more unique.&#8221;</p><footer>Statement of his philosophy, in R. A. Nicholson's introduction to The Secrets of the Self (1920) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57317/pg57317-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
